\centering
\small
\caption{SciERC NER epoch curve (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Training duration modulates both instance degeneracy and within-instance LP-quality alignment. ``Degen.'': constant-F1 instance degeneracy (gold-filtered). ``LP $\Delta$'': LP selection gain over greedy (gold-filtered; none reaches the ${\approx}$1.5~pp MDE). ``$\rho$ med'': median within-instance LP-quality correlation. Greedy F1 uses span-based evaluation consistent with Tables 1--3; $\rho$ and LP $\Delta$ use text-based matching consistent with Table~\ref{tab:within_rho}.}
\label{tab:epoch_scierc}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Metric & 3-epoch & 5-epoch & 10-epoch \\
\midrule
Greedy F1 & 0.650 & 0.644 & 0.649 \\
Instance degen.\ (\%) & 6.4 & 17.8 & 47.8 \\
LP $\Delta$ (pp) & $-$1.24 & $+$0.64 & $+$1.02 \\
Within-inst.\ $\rho$ (median) & 0.319 & 0.262 & 0.112 \\
Within-inst.\ $\rho$ (mean) & 0.273 & 0.179 & 0.066 \\
\% positive $\rho$ & 76.4 & 64.1 & 54.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scope of epoch analysis.}
The Few-NERD epoch analysis covers 2 points (3 vs.\ 5 epochs); the SciERC curve provides a 3-point gradient showing monotonic degeneracy increase with simultaneous within-instance alignment degradation.
CoNLL epoch variation remains unverified; high-degeneracy datasets (CoNLL at 59--89\%) may exhibit a ceiling effect where additional training has limited marginal impact.
All Few-NERD results in the main text use the 3-epoch model (standard early stopping); the 5-epoch comparison is a controlled ablation.
All SciERC results use the 5-epoch model; the 3- and 10-epoch variants serve as controlled ablations.

\subsection{Degeneracy-Aware LP Selection}\label{app:dals}

The non-tied subset analysis (Table~\ref{tab:nontied_main}) identifies within-instance LP range (max minus min LP across $N$ samples) as a per-instance degeneracy proxy: instances with near-zero LP range carry no discriminative signal.
Degeneracy-aware LP selection (DALS) operationalizes this: when LP range exceeds $\tau{=}0.05$ (the threshold independently motivated by the non-tied analysis; Appendix~\ref{app:epsilon_robustness}), select the highest-LP sample; otherwise fall back to greedy.
On SciERC, DALS yields $+0.71{\pm}0.48$~pp (3-seed mean; individual seeds: $+0.19$ to $+1.14$~pp); on Few-NERD, $+0.30$~pp ($p{<}0.001$; gain remains positive across $\tau \in [0.01, 0.15]$); on CoNLL, where pure LP selection degrades F1 ($-0.85$~pp), DALS shows comparable degradation (Qwen: $-0.87$~pp; LLaMA: neutral).
DALS improves over pure LP selection when degeneracy is moderate, provides partial damage control when LP selection is harmful, and defaults to greedy when LP range signals no information.
While gains are modest and seed-sensitive, LP range serves as an operationally accessible degeneracy proxy, though the mixed cross-dataset results confirm that degeneracy gating alone cannot ensure positive selection outcomes.

\paragraph{LOO $\tau$ sensitivity.}
Leave-one-out (LOO) cross-validation across datasets shows that the optimal $\tau$ spans two orders of magnitude: $\tau^*{=}0.001$ on Few-NERD, $0.05$ on SciERC, and $0.10$ on CoNLL.
LOO regret (the gap between oracle and LOO-selected $\tau$) ranges from 0.51 to 1.21~pp.
The best single cross-dataset $\tau$ ($\tau{=}0.05$) matches the independently motivated non-tied threshold, but yields dataset-specific suboptimality: even with oracle $\tau$, CoNLL remains at $-0.31$~pp.
This sensitivity reinforces the diagnostic framing of DALS: per-dataset $\tau$ tuning is required for positive gains, and even optimally tuned DALS cannot overcome weak LP-quality alignment on high-degeneracy datasets.

\subsection{Conditional Decomposition of LP Selection}\label{app:conditional_decomp}

To test whether LP-quality alignment is associated with selection gains within datasets, we restrict LP selection to the favorable subset: non-degenerate instances whose within-instance LP range exceeds the dataset-specific median (SciERC: 0.040 nats, CoNLL: 0.062, Few-NERD: 0.067).
Table~\ref{tab:conditional_decomp} reports results across three datasets.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection on the favorable subset (non-degenerate instances with LP range above the dataset-specific median) per dataset (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). CI: bootstrap 95\% confidence interval.}
\label{tab:conditional_decomp}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Dataset & LP $\Delta$ (pp) & 95\% CI & Interpretation \\
\midrule
SciERC & $+$2.47 & [$+$0.30, $+$4.70] & significant positive \\
Few-NERD & $+$0.63 & [$-$0.02, $+$1.30] & marginal positive \\
CoNLL & $-$1.78 & [$-$2.83, $-$0.76] & significant negative \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

On SciERC, restricting to the favorable subset recovers a significant LP gain, consistent with LP-quality alignment predicting selection within the non-degenerate pool.
On Few-NERD, the trend is directionally consistent but the confidence interval crosses zero; the full-dataset result ($+1.39$~pp, $p{<}0.001$) reflects the larger sample size rather than a stronger per-instance effect.
CoNLL remains negative even on the favorable subset, consistent with the ceiling-regime interpretation: at $>$90\% greedy F1, residual errors are idiosyncratic and LP cannot rank corrections.

\subsection{Power Analysis for Cross-Dataset Degeneracy--LP Correlation}\label{app:power_analysis}

Table~\ref{tab:power_analysis} reports post-hoc power for the $n{=}23$ cross-dataset correlation between degeneracy and LP selection gain ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Post-hoc power analysis ($n{=}23$, two-tailed, $\alpha{=}0.05$). At this sample size, only large correlations ($\rho{\geq}0.5$) are detectable with adequate power.}
\label{tab:power_analysis}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
True $\rho$ & Power at $n{=}23$ \\
\midrule
0.1 & 0.07 \\
0.2 & 0.13 \\
0.3 & 0.25 \\
0.4 & 0.44 \\
0.5 & 0.65 \\
0.6 & 0.84 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\noindent 80\% power requires $n{\approx}85$ for $\rho{=}0.3$ and $n{\approx}47$ for $\rho{=}0.5$. The null result should be interpreted as absence of evidence for a large effect rather than evidence of absence.

\subsection{Entity-Token LP Decomposition}\label{app:entity_token_lp}

Full-sequence LP averages over all output tokens, diluting entity-relevant signal with formatting and schema tokens.
Entity-token LP restricts the average to tokens within entity text and type spans, yielding $4{\times}$ higher within-instance variance (median 0.000644 vs.\ 0.000161).
Table~\ref{tab:entity_token_lp} compares the two aggregation strategies on SciERC (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Full-sequence vs.\ entity-token LP on SciERC NER (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Entity-token LP has higher variance and stronger correlation, yet selection degrades.}
\label{tab:entity_token_lp}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Aggregation & Med.\ var. & $\rho$ & AUROC & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Full-sequence & 0.000161 & 0.297 & 0.642 & $+$0.35 \\
Entity text+type & 0.000644 & 0.440 & 0.719 & $-$0.73 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

Despite stronger correlation ($+$48\%) and higher AUROC, entity-token LP produces worse selection, reinforcing that compression operates at the representation level rather than token aggregation: the correlation-selection gap persists even with a more targeted signal.

\subsection{Entity-Level Majority Voting}\label{app:entity_merge}

Entity-level majority voting constructs a merged output by including all (text, type) entity tuples that appear in at least $k$ of $N$ samples (with $k = N/2$ as the default majority threshold).
Unlike best-of-$N$ selection (which picks one complete sample), merge combines evidence across samples at entity granularity.
We sweep $k \in \{2, 3, \ldots, 8\}$ on each dataset (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$) and report the best threshold per dataset.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Entity-level majority voting results (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). ``Greedy'': $T{=}0$ baseline. ``Best merge'': optimal $k$ per dataset from sweep $k \in \{2, \ldots, 8\}$. ``$\Delta$'': merge F1 minus greedy. ``Recovery'': $\Delta$ / (oracle $-$ greedy). Only SciERC shows positive recovery; even with oracle $k$, CoNLL and Few-NERD degrade.}
\label{tab:entity_merge}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Dataset & Greedy & Best merge ($k^*$) & $\Delta$ (pp) & Recovery (\%) \\
\midrule
SciERC & 0.644 & 0.660 ($k{=}3$) & $+$1.57 & $+$11.9 \\
CoNLL & 0.907 & 0.903 ($k{=}3$) & $-$0.38 & $-$9.0 \\
Few-NERD & 0.748 & 0.744 ($k{=}2$) & $-$0.43 & $-$3.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

On degenerate subsets (instances where all $N$ samples yield identical F1), merge is uniformly negative across all three datasets, confirming that entity-level aggregation cannot bypass the diversity bottleneck identified in the main text (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

